---
title: LVM
order: 4
description: Logical Volume Manager — pvcreate, vgcreate, lvcreate, lvextend
tags: [disk, lvm, pvcreate, vgcreate, lvcreate, lvextend, pvs, vgs, lvs]
---

LVM provides flexible disk management with physical volumes (PV), volume groups (VG), and logical volumes (LV). Volumes can be resized, moved, and snapshotted without unmounting.

@text
LVM hierarchy: Physical Volumes (PV) -> Volume Groups (VG) -> Logical Volumes (LV).

Inspect existing LVM configuration.

@code bash
# Summary views
sudo pvs          # physical volumes
sudo vgs          # volume groups
sudo lvs          # logical volumes

# Detailed views
sudo pvdisplay
sudo vgdisplay
sudo lvdisplay

# Detailed info for a specific item
sudo pvdisplay /dev/sda1
sudo vgdisplay myvg
sudo lvdisplay /dev/myvg/mylv

# Scan for all LVM devices
sudo pvscan
sudo vgscan
sudo lvscan

@text
Create LVM from scratch — full workflow.

@code bash
# 1. Initialize physical volumes on partitions or whole disks
sudo pvcreate /dev/sda1
sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb1

# 2. Create a volume group from one or more PVs
sudo vgcreate myvg /dev/sda1

# Create VG with specific physical extent size
sudo vgcreate -s 16M myvg /dev/sda1

# 3. Create logical volumes
# Fixed size
sudo lvcreate -L 50G -n data myvg

# Use percentage of free space
sudo lvcreate -l 100%FREE -n data myvg

# Use percentage of total VG size
sudo lvcreate -l 80%VG -n data myvg

# 4. Create filesystem on the LV
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/myvg/data

# 5. Mount it
sudo mount /dev/myvg/data /mnt/data

# fstab entry (use /dev/mapper/ path or UUID)
# /dev/mapper/myvg-data   /mnt/data   ext4   defaults   0  2

@text
Extend and modify volumes.

@code bash
# Add a new PV to an existing VG
sudo pvcreate /dev/sdc1
sudo vgextend myvg /dev/sdc1

# Extend LV by a specific amount
sudo lvextend -L +20G /dev/myvg/data

# Extend LV to a specific size
sudo lvextend -L 100G /dev/myvg/data

# Extend LV to fill all free space in VG
sudo lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/myvg/data

# Extend LV and resize filesystem in one step (-r flag)
sudo lvextend -r -L +20G /dev/myvg/data

# Resize filesystem after extending (if -r not used)
sudo resize2fs /dev/myvg/data       # ext4
sudo xfs_growfs /mnt/data            # xfs (by mountpoint)

@text
Shrink volumes (ext4 only; XFS cannot shrink).

@code bash
# Shrink requires unmounting first
sudo umount /mnt/data

# Check filesystem
sudo e2fsck -f /dev/myvg/data

# Shrink filesystem first
sudo resize2fs /dev/myvg/data 30G

# Then shrink the LV to match
sudo lvreduce -L 30G /dev/myvg/data

# Or shrink both in one step
sudo lvreduce -r -L 30G /dev/myvg/data

# Remount
sudo mount /dev/myvg/data /mnt/data

@text
LVM snapshots — point-in-time copies.

@code bash
# Create a snapshot (allocate space for changes)
sudo lvcreate -s -L 10G -n data_snap /dev/myvg/data

# Mount snapshot read-only
sudo mount -o ro /dev/myvg/data_snap /mnt/snapshot

# Remove snapshot when done
sudo umount /mnt/snapshot
sudo lvremove /dev/myvg/data_snap

@text
Remove LVM components (reverse order: LV -> VG -> PV).

@code bash
# Unmount and remove logical volume
sudo umount /mnt/data
sudo lvremove /dev/myvg/data

# Remove volume group
sudo vgremove myvg

# Remove physical volume label
sudo pvremove /dev/sda1

@text
Move data off a physical volume (before removing a disk).

@code bash
# Move all extents from one PV to others in the same VG
sudo pvmove /dev/sda1

# Move to a specific PV
sudo pvmove /dev/sda1 /dev/sdb1

# Then remove the emptied PV from the VG
sudo vgreduce myvg /dev/sda1
sudo pvremove /dev/sda1
